 The record **types** of the multipart commit protocol (proposal 0016, still a draft) are landed ahead of their writers, under disjoint key prefixes: the `mpuctl` admission ledger singleton (a `Budget` profile and the session count it derives `MAX_SESSIONS` from), and per-upload `mpu:<id>` (a session's target, lifecycle state and epoch, and its segment-group nonce — stored in every state from Create to the terminal delete, so the batch that ends a `Completing` attempt can name that attempt's segment records, the nonce at the attempt's fence epoch, and the terminal delete can name the group's reservation marker even for an upload that never reached Complete; proposal 0016's `mpu:` row does not list the field yet), `slot:<id>:<k>` (an in-flight part reservation), `part:<id>:<n>` (a committed part's chunk list, length and digest) and `psum:<id>:<n>` (that part's summary), and the reclamation pair `retire:bytes:<token>` (bytes to orphan-mark, then the records that named them) and `retire:records:<token>` (records to delete whose bytes something else still protects), whose value names what one obligation owes — a session's staged residue, a range-encoded set of its part numbers, one part attempt's chunk list, a superseded generation (by the chunk list a flat root carried, or by the segment group a segmented one named — the two shapes a published map has, never both) or a rolled-back completion attempt's segment records, in the combinations the protocol installs as single obligations — every one validated structurally at decode (ADR-0045) rather than trusted on successful JSON parsing alone, so a stored record whose fields disagree with each other (a session whose publish target names another object or another fence epoch, a part whose length is not its chunks' summed length, a slot born already lapsed) is a typed error rather than a live value. A retirement obligation's identity lives partly in its `<token>` — which session at which fence epoch, which part attempt of it, which superseded generation — so its value is decoded **against its own key**, and a payload whose mode, token scope, generation identity or segment epoch disagrees with the token naming it is likewise a typed error rather than an obligation that would reclaim one attempt's bytes while clearing another's. The owned staging entry `sidx:<id>:<n>:<chunk>` is the lease record a streaming write stores under `pending:<chunk>`, plus the owning upload id (`owner`) and the chunk's planned placement (`staged` — an erasure scheme the coder must support, and a D server per planned fragment, a count that decode leaves unchecked as for a committed chunk). Both are present on an owned entry and absent on an ordinary lease, and each is omitted when absent, so a `pending:` record written before them decodes and re-encodes byte-identically — load-bearing because a renewal puts a freshly encoded entry over the bytes it read. A value whose fields disagree with each other or with its key (one field alone, an owned entry under `pending:`, an ordinary lease under `sidx:`, an owner other than the key's) is refused by every reader and both `pending:` writers, and an expiry sweep skips it without reclaiming its chunk; GC's expired-lease input, off unless an operator arms it, also names it on the audit seam, and the post-restore reconciliation holds its chunk unmarked and reports the entry as needing a human, as it does an unreadable chunk map. An ownership field spelled `null` is refused too: the encoder never emits that spelling, so a lease that carried it would be rewritten by its next renewal. Nothing writes or consumes these records in production yet — the protocol itself (fenced state transitions, staged publication, retirement) arrives with the store round trip (#656–#659) and is specified in the proposal, not here. Because every transition it specifies is a compare-and-set on the record's exact bytes, the decoders enforce serialization identity now, before any writer exists to depend on it: the accepted set is exactly the encoder's image, byte for byte — an absent `content_type` is spelled only by omitting the field (the inode trio's omitted-when-absent rule), a noncanonical spelling of an accepted value (fields reordered, whitespace inserted) is a decode error rather than bytes a CAS could never match, and a part record's chunk values are read through a closed wire shape of their own, so an unknown or omitted chunk field is a decode error rather than a silent rewrite.
 
+The post-restore reconciliation keeps one more singleton beside them, `mpufence`: the **restore-fence generation** that proposal 0016 requires to complete before any gateway serves multipart verbs on a restored image (0016 names the generation but gives it no key). Its value is `{"generation":N,"complete":B}`, closed and canonical like `mpuctl`'s. `generation` numbers that pass's runs from 1, so the record is absent until the first run, and absent is the only spelling of "no run has happened". `complete` says whether that run finished its session fence. Each run first writes the next generation as not complete, and marks or fences nothing until that write is acknowledged. After its last write it records the generation complete, but only if every write it made was acknowledged and no upload session is left for a human: one it could not read or fence, or a fenced one whose segment records need a human. Every run judges those sessions afresh from their records, so a session an earlier run fenced but could not clear holds back every later run until someone repairs it. Findings that are not about a session, such as a lost or misplaced chunk or an unreadable object or staged record, do not hold the generation back. Both writes are conditioned on the bytes last read there, so a completion that lands late never overwrites a newer run's generation. What the record cannot tell: a restore rewinds it with everything else, so an image captured after an earlier run completed comes back reading complete until the next run starts. No gateway reads it yet. The gateway gate (#508) needs a restore-scoped signal beside it, and until then what keeps multipart off a restored image is the runbook's order: the reconciliation runs with writers stopped, before any gateway is re-enabled.
+
 The protocol's client-visible answers, and the two digests a completed upload is identified by, are landed the same way: as pure functions that no production path calls yet. A multipart object's ETag is composed on ADR-0047's SHA-256 basis, never MD5: the lowercase-hex SHA-256 of the named parts' raw 32-byte digests concatenated in part-number order, then `-` and the number of parts named (`<hex>-<N>`, with `N` from 1 to 999,999, the most parts the `part:` key grammar can address). A `Completed` session record's `etag` holds that ETag whole, suffix included, and decode refuses any other spelling (a bare digest, a count out of range, a noncanonical number), because by the time the session's tombstone answers a retry, the part records the ETag was composed from may already be retired. Beside it, `complete_fingerprint` is a SHA-256 over each named part's number (4 bytes, big-endian) followed by its digest, so the tombstone answers an identical retry with the recorded ETag and a different part list under the consumed upload id with `NoSuchUpload`. Both digests are computed only over a non-empty list in strictly ascending part-number order; any other list is refused, never sorted. Proposal 0016's decision-3 table answers its five verbs (`UploadPart`, `CompleteMultipartUpload`, `AbortMultipartUpload`, `ListParts`, `ListMultipartUploads`) in each session state and for an absent record, in a typed outcome vocabulary that names no HTTP status; the S3 status and XML mapping arrives with #508.
 
 This makes rename a single dirent mutation (atomic under the same mechanism as a write) instead of a mass key rewrite, and makes cross-zone sharing expressible (a dirent pointing at an inode owned elsewhere). It is the strongest concrete driver of the requirement that the metadata store offer an **atomic multi-key transaction**, because file creation must atomically write both the inode and its dirent. That requirement is what disqualified HBase-class stores and what every backend choice since has had to satisfy; the backend satisfying it in production is now FoundationDB (ADR-0042, superseding ADR-0008's TiKV). See section 6.
